IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `scja-core`

-- scja3-s80-s82-rules-and-conversion.l4: sections 80 and 82 (what in them decides something for a person).
-- Layer 1, written from the text alone (SCJA1969.txt lines 5016-5338, 5345-5353 and 5373-5461).
-- Section 80 is the Rules of Court power: its heads (subsection (1), (2)(a) to (i), (k) to (r), (2A), (2B)) are rule-making machinery
-- and are coverage rows with the reason "machinery"; subsection (3), the make-up of the Rules Committee, is encoded as a small table because PLAN.md lists it among the definitions of this range; subsection (2)(j) (the 8% cap on interest) and subsections (4) to (6) decide something and are encoded.
-- Section 81 (the yearly assembly of the Council of Judges) and section 83(1) (the Minister's power to amend six of the Schedules) are encoded as one function each, so that a reader who asks "must the Council meet this year" or "may the Minister amend this Schedule" gets the text's answer;
-- the purposes of the Council ((a) to (c)), subsection 83(1A) and subsection 83(2) say what the Council considers or what an amending order may contain, and decide nothing for a person: coverage rows with the reason "machinery".

§ `Section 80: Rules of Court`

-- src:5133 | (j) regulating the rate of interest payable on all debts,
-- src:5134 | including judgment debts, or on the sums found due on
-- src:5135 | taking accounts between parties, or on sums found due and
-- src:5136 | unpaid by receivers or other persons liable to account to the
-- src:5137 | court, except that the rate of interest must not in any case
-- src:5138 | exceed 8% per annum, unless it has been otherwise agreed
-- src:5139 | between the parties;
@ref s 80(2)(j) (src:5133-5139)
-- "8% per annum" is the Act's own figure.
`s 80(2)(j) the Act's maximum rate of interest per annum` MEANS 8

GIVEN `the rate of interest per annum, as a percentage` IS A NUMBER
      `the parties have otherwise agreed the rate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 80(2)(j) the Rules of Court may regulate the rate of interest at that rate` MEANS
    IF `the rate of interest per annum, as a percentage` AT MOST `s 80(2)(j) the Act's maximum rate of interest per annum`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF `the parties have otherwise agreed the rate`
         -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the cap applies "unless it has been otherwise agreed between the parties", and the text grants nothing where the parties have agreed
         THEN NOTHING
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the rate of interest must not in any case exceed 8% per annum, unless it has been otherwise agreed between the parties"
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:5133 | (j) regulating the rate of interest payable on all debts,
-- src:5134 | including judgment debts, or on the sums found due on
-- src:5135 | taking accounts between parties, or on sums found due and
-- src:5136 | unpaid by receivers or other persons liable to account to the
-- src:5137 | court, except that the rate of interest must not in any case
-- src:5138 | exceed 8% per annum, unless it has been otherwise agreed
-- src:5139 | between the parties;
@ref s 80(2)(j) (the cap) (src:5133-5139)
GIVEN `the rate of interest per annum, as a percentage` IS A NUMBER
      `the parties have otherwise agreed the rate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 80(2)(j) the cap of the Act's maximum rate bars the rate of interest` MEANS
    IF `the rate of interest per annum, as a percentage` AT MOST `s 80(2)(j) the Act's maximum rate of interest per annum`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the rate of interest must not in any case exceed 8% per annum", and a rate at or below it does not exceed it (fork S3-B6)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `the parties have otherwise agreed the rate`
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the cap holds "unless it has been otherwise agreed between the parties", so where they have agreed the cap does not bar the rate (fork S3-B6)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- src:5306 | (3) The Rules Committee consists of —
-- src:5308 | (a) the Chief Justice, who is the Chairperson of the
-- src:5309 | Committee;
-- src:5311 | (b) the Attorney-General;
-- src:5313 | (c) not more than 5 Supreme Court Judges (excluding the
-- src:5314 | Presiding Judge of the State Courts) to be appointed by the
-- src:5315 | Chief Justice for such period as the Chief Justice may
-- src:5316 | specify in writing;
-- src:5318 | (d) the Presiding Judge of the State Courts;
-- src:5320 | (e) a District Judge to be appointed by the Chief Justice for
-- src:5321 | such period as the Chief Justice may specify in writing; and
-- src:5323 | (f) 2 practising advocates and solicitors to be appointed by the
-- src:5324 | Chief Justice for such period as the Chief Justice may
-- src:5325 | specify in writing.
@ref s 80(3) (src:5306-5325)
-- The Rules Committee "consists of" six kinds of member.  The text gives a head-count for three of them: "not more than 5" Supreme Court Judges, "a District Judge" (one), "2" practising advocates and solicitors.
DECLARE `s 80(3) kind of member of the Rules Committee` IS ONE OF
    `s 80(3)(a) the Chief Justice, who is the Chairperson of the Committee`
    `s 80(3)(b) the Attorney-General`
    `s 80(3)(c) a Supreme Court Judge (excluding the Presiding Judge of the State Courts) appointed by the Chief Justice`
    `s 80(3)(d) the Presiding Judge of the State Courts`
    `s 80(3)(e) a District Judge appointed by the Chief Justice`
    `s 80(3)(f) a practising advocate and solicitor appointed by the Chief Justice`

-- Whether the Rules Committee may have this many members of the kind.  "Consists of" lists the members and closes the list (a marginal reading, see NOTES.md): a head-count the text does not give is the text's no,
-- except that "not more than 5" Supreme Court Judges leaves 0 to 5 open.
GIVEN kind IS A `s 80(3) kind of member of the Rules Committee`
      `the number of members of that kind` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 80(3) the Rules Committee has this number of members of the kind` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `s 80(3)(a) the Chief Justice, who is the Chairperson of the Committee` THEN
        IF `the number of members of that kind` EQUALS 1
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Rules Committee consists of — (a) the Chief Justice, who is the Chairperson of the Committee"
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN `s 80(3)(b) the Attorney-General` THEN
        IF `the number of members of that kind` EQUALS 1
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Rules Committee consists of — (b) the Attorney-General"
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN `s 80(3)(c) a Supreme Court Judge (excluding the Presiding Judge of the State Courts) appointed by the Chief Justice` THEN
        IF `the number of members of that kind` AT MOST 5
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(c) not more than 5 Supreme Court Judges (excluding the Presiding Judge of the State Courts)"
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN `s 80(3)(d) the Presiding Judge of the State Courts` THEN
        IF `the number of members of that kind` EQUALS 1
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Rules Committee consists of — (d) the Presiding Judge of the State Courts"
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN `s 80(3)(e) a District Judge appointed by the Chief Justice` THEN
        IF `the number of members of that kind` EQUALS 1
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(e) a District Judge to be appointed by the Chief Justice"
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHEN `s 80(3)(f) a practising advocate and solicitor appointed by the Chief Justice` THEN
        IF `the number of members of that kind` EQUALS 2
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(f) 2 practising advocates and solicitors to be appointed by the Chief Justice"
        ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:5329 | (4) At any meeting of the Rules Committee, 5 members form a
-- src:5330 | quorum and all questions are to be decided by a majority of votes of
-- src:5331 | the members present and voting.
@ref s 80(4) (src:5329-5331)
-- "5 members" is the Act's own figure.
`s 80(4) the Act's number of members that form a quorum` MEANS 5

GIVEN `the number of members of the Rules Committee present` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 80(4) the meeting of the Rules Committee has a quorum` MEANS
    IF `the number of members of the Rules Committee present` AT LEAST `s 80(4) the Act's number of members that form a quorum`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "5 members form a quorum"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:5329 | (4) At any meeting of the Rules Committee, 5 members form a
-- src:5330 | quorum and all questions are to be decided by a majority of votes of
-- src:5331 | the members present and voting.
@ref s 80(4) (majority) (src:5329-5331)
GIVEN `the number of members present and voting for the question` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of members present and voting against the question` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 80(4) the question is decided by a majority of the votes of the members present and voting, as the section requires` MEANS
    IF `the number of members present and voting for the question` GREATER THAN `the number of members present and voting against the question`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "all questions are to be decided by a majority of votes of the members present and voting", and the text does not say what follows for a question that lacks the majority (fork S3-F7)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:5333 | (5) No Rules of Court may be made without the consent of the
-- src:5334 | Chief Justice.
@ref s 80(5) (src:5333-5334)
GIVEN `the Chief Justice has consented to the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 80(5) the Rules of Court may be made` MEANS
    IF `the Chief Justice has consented to the Rules of Court`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar is "without the consent of the Chief Justice", and the text grants nothing where there is consent
    THEN NOTHING
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "No Rules of Court may be made without the consent of the Chief Justice."
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:5333 | (5) No Rules of Court may be made without the consent of the
-- src:5334 | Chief Justice.
@ref s 80(5) (the bar) (src:5333-5334)
GIVEN `the Chief Justice has consented to the Rules of Court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 80(5) the bar on making the Rules of Court applies` MEANS
    IF `the Chief Justice has consented to the Rules of Court`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): the bar is "without the consent of the Chief Justice", so with the consent it does not apply (fork S3-B6)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- src:5336 | (6) All Rules of Court made under this section must be presented to
-- src:5337 | Parliament as soon as possible after publication in the Gazette.
@ref s 80(6) (src:5336-5337)
GIVEN `the Rules of Court were made under section 80` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 80(6) the Rules of Court must be presented to Parliament as soon as possible after publication in the Gazette` MEANS
    IF `the Rules of Court were made under section 80`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the duty is for "All Rules of Court made under this section"
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 82: conversion of pending petitions and motions`

-- Helper: whether a list of conditions all hold.
GIVEN conditions IS A LIST OF BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 82(1) all of the conditions hold` MEANS
    NOT (elem FALSE conditions)

-- src:5376 | 82.—(1) Where —
-- src:5378 | (a) under any written law any civil action or application may
-- src:5379 | be commenced in or made to the Court of Appeal, a Judge
-- src:5380 | of Appeal, the High Court, a Judge or the Registrar (called
-- src:5381 | in this section the Court); and
-- src:5383 | (b) the provisions under any written law by virtue of which
-- src:5384 | such an action or application was required to be
-- src:5385 | commenced or made by way of a petition, a motion or
-- src:5386 | an originating motion have been amended so as to require
-- src:5387 | that any such action or application must, as from the date
-- src:5388 | appointed for the coming into operation of the amendment,
-- src:5389 | be commenced or made by way of a writ of summons, an
-- src:5390 | originating summons or a summons,
-- src:5392 | then, if any such action or application that has been commenced or
-- src:5393 | made before that date by way of a petition, a motion or an originating
-- src:5394 | motion is still pending before the Court on or after that date, the Court
-- src:5395 | may, if it thinks just and expedient, order that the action or application
-- src:5396 | (called in this section a pending action or application) be converted to
-- src:5397 | and be continued as an action or application commenced or made by
-- src:5404 | way of a writ of summons, an originating summons or a summons, as
-- src:5405 | is appropriate.
@ref s 82(1) (src:5376-5405)
GIVEN `under a written law a civil action or application may be commenced in or made to the Court of Appeal, a Judge of Appeal, the High Court, a Judge or the Registrar` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provisions requiring commencement by petition, motion or originating motion have been amended to require a writ of summons, originating summons or summons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the action or application was commenced or made before the date of the amendment by petition, motion or originating motion` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the action or application is still pending before the Court on or after that date` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court thinks it just and expedient` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 82(1) the Court may order that the pending action or application be converted and continued` MEANS
    IF `s 82(1) all of the conditions hold` (LIST
              `under a written law a civil action or application may be commenced in or made to the Court of Appeal, a Judge of Appeal, the High Court, a Judge or the Registrar`
            , `the provisions requiring commencement by petition, motion or originating motion have been amended to require a writ of summons, originating summons or summons`
            , `the action or application was commenced or made before the date of the amendment by petition, motion or originating motion`
            , `the action or application is still pending before the Court on or after that date`
            , `the Court thinks it just and expedient`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where (a) ... and (b) ..., then, if any such action or application ... is still pending before the Court on or after that date, the Court may, if it thinks just and expedient, order that the action or application ... be converted"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:5407 | (2) The Chief Justice may, where he or she considers it necessary or
-- src:5408 | expedient to improve efficiency in the administration of justice, by
-- src:5409 | order direct that any class or description of pending actions or
-- src:5410 | applications before the Court be converted to and be continued as
-- src:5411 | actions or applications commenced or made by way of a writ of
-- src:5412 | summons, an originating summons or a summons, as is appropriate.
@ref s 82(2) (src:5407-5412)
GIVEN `the Chief Justice considers it necessary or expedient to improve efficiency in the administration of justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 82(2) the Chief Justice may by order direct that a class or description of pending actions or applications be converted` MEANS
    IF `the Chief Justice considers it necessary or expedient to improve efficiency in the administration of justice`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where he or she considers it necessary or expedient to improve efficiency in the administration of justice"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:5414 | (3) Where pursuant to subsection (1) or (2) any pending action or
-- src:5415 | application has been converted to an action or application
-- src:5416 | commenced or made by way of a writ of summons, an originating
-- src:5417 | summons or a summons —
-- src:5419 | (a) the action or application is to be continued in accordance
-- src:5420 | with the provisions of the relevant written law and the
-- src:5421 | practice and procedure as are in force and applicable in
-- src:5422 | relation to that action or application at the time of the
-- src:5423 | conversion; and
@ref s 82(3)(a) (src:5414-5423)
GIVEN `a pending action or application has been converted pursuant to subsection (1) or (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 82(3)(a) the action or application is to be continued under the relevant written law and the practice and procedure in force at the time of the conversion` MEANS
    IF `a pending action or application has been converted pursuant to subsection (1) or (2)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where pursuant to subsection (1) or (2) any pending action or application has been converted"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- src:5414 | (3) Where pursuant to subsection (1) or (2) any pending action or
-- src:5415 | application has been converted to an action or application
-- src:5416 | commenced or made by way of a writ of summons, an originating
-- src:5417 | summons or a summons —
-- src:5419 | (a) the action or application is to be continued in accordance
-- src:5420 | with the provisions of the relevant written law and the
-- src:5421 | practice and procedure as are in force and applicable in
-- src:5422 | relation to that action or application at the time of the
-- src:5423 | conversion; and
-- src:5425 | (b) the Court may give to the parties to the action or
-- src:5426 | application such directions as to the conduct and costs of
-- src:5427 | the action or application as it thinks just and expedient for
-- src:5428 | the purpose of facilitating the conversion of the action or
-- src:5429 | application to an action or application commenced or made
-- src:5430 | by way of a writ of summons, an originating summons or a
-- src:5431 | summons (as the case may be) and its continuance as such.
@ref s 82(3)(b) (src:5414-5431)
GIVEN `a pending action or application has been converted pursuant to subsection (1) or (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 82(3)(b) the Court may give the parties directions as to the conduct and costs of the action or application` MEANS
    IF `a pending action or application has been converted pursuant to subsection (1) or (2)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where pursuant to subsection (1) or (2) any pending action or application has been converted"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `Section 81: the Council of Judges`

-- src:5345 | 81. A council of the Supreme Court Judges is to assemble at least
-- src:5346 | once in every year, on such day or days as may be fixed by the Chief
-- src:5347 | Justice, for the purpose of —
@ref s 81 (src:5345-5347)
GIVEN `the number of times the council of the Supreme Court Judges has assembled in the year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 81 the council of the Supreme Court Judges has assembled in the year as the section requires` MEANS
    IF `the number of times the council of the Supreme Court Judges has assembled in the year` AT LEAST 1
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "A council of the Supreme Court Judges is to assemble at least once in every year", and the text does not say what follows when it does not (a bare "is to" not done; fork S3-F7)
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 83: the Minister's power to amend the Schedules`

-- src:5435 | 83.—(1) The Minister may, after consulting the Chief Justice, by
-- src:5436 | order in the Gazette, amend the Third, Fourth, Fifth, Sixth, Seventh or
-- src:5437 | Ninth Schedule.
@ref s 83(1) (src:5435-5437)
DECLARE `s 83 Schedule` IS ONE OF
    `s 83 the First Schedule`
    `s 83 the Second Schedule`
    `s 83 the Third Schedule`
    `s 83 the Fourth Schedule`
    `s 83 the Fifth Schedule`
    `s 83 the Sixth Schedule`
    `s 83 the Seventh Schedule`
    `s 83 the Eighth Schedule`
    `s 83 the Ninth Schedule`
    `s 83 the Tenth Schedule`

GIVEN schedule IS A `s 83 Schedule`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 83(1) the Schedule is one that the Minister may amend by order` MEANS
    schedule EQUALS `s 83 the Third Schedule`
    OR schedule EQUALS `s 83 the Fourth Schedule`
    OR schedule EQUALS `s 83 the Fifth Schedule`
    OR schedule EQUALS `s 83 the Sixth Schedule`
    OR schedule EQUALS `s 83 the Seventh Schedule`
    OR schedule EQUALS `s 83 the Ninth Schedule`

GIVEN schedule IS A `s 83 Schedule`
      `the Minister has consulted the Chief Justice` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amendment is made by order in the Gazette` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83(1) the Minister may amend the Schedule` MEANS
    IF `s 83(1) the Schedule is one that the Minister may amend by order` schedule
    THEN IF `the Minister has consulted the Chief Justice` AND `the amendment is made by order in the Gazette`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Minister may, after consulting the Chief Justice, by order in the Gazette, amend the Third, Fourth, Fifth, Sixth, Seventh or Ninth Schedule": without the consulting or the order in the Gazette the power is not exercised as the subsection gives it (fork S3-B9, "after consulting")
         ELSE JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 83(1) names "the Third, Fourth, Fifth, Sixth, Seventh or Ninth Schedule" and the text gives this power over no other Schedule; another source may
    ELSE NOTHING
